## 1. Executive Summary & Epistemic Grounding

This audit provides a strictly read-only, evidence-grounded comparison between the primary literature for the Mode-I benchmark (**Pandey & Kumar, 2025**) and the local Abaqus finite element user-element implementations (historical baseline Job **`1398090.mmaster02`** and harmonized candidate Job **`1401091.mmaster02`**).

### Governing Project Directives Preserved
1. **Authoritative Project Fixed-Mesh Anchor:**
   Job `1398090.mmaster02` ($F_{\text{peak}} = 0.757778\,\mathrm{kN}, u_{\text{peak}} = 0.005857\,\mathrm{mm}, K_0 = 138.0956\,\mathrm{kN/mm}$) remains the **governing project fixed-mesh anchor**. It is neither replaced nor discarded.
2. **Preservation of Governing Literature Reference Target:**
   The project record defines approximately **$0.758\,\mathrm{kN}$ at $0.005860\,\mathrm{mm}$** as the active literature check target. In accordance with supervisor instructions, these values are **not** arbitrarily retired or discarded based solely on an AI raster re-digitization. Instead, newly extracted values ($0.734831\,\mathrm{kN}$ and $0.720506\,\mathrm{kN}$) are formally recorded as **conflicting digitization findings requiring human/supervisor reconciliation**.
3. **No Unwarranted Assumptions on Published Data:**
   All unsupported specificity (e.g., claiming the paper used `CPS4`/`CPS3`, `CPE4`/`CPE3`, plane strain, duplicate-node seams, or explicit $d=0$ field initialization) has been purged. Explicit text statements are strictly distinguished from visual figure interpretations.
4. **Energy Split Discrepancy Verified:**
   Pandey & Kumar explicitly specify for this Mode-I example the *"anisotropic strain energy decomposition of Miehe et al. [32]"*. The local subroutine (`f42_mixed_uel.for`) implements Molnár's isotropic mechanical degradation and un-split deviatoric driving energy. This is formally classified as **`IMPLEMENTATION_DIFFERENCE_VERIFIED`**.
5. **Separation of Discretizations:**
   The published Standard PFM mesh (26,282 mixed quad/tri elements, global $0.02\,\mathrm{mm}$, local $0.003\,\mathrm{mm}$), the proposed adaptive mesh (13,941 mixed quad/tri elements, global $0.02\,\mathrm{mm}$, local $0.001\,\mathrm{mm}$), and the local structured mesh (15,192 pure quadrilateral elements) are treated as three distinct topological discretizations.

