## 6. Analysis of Implementation Differences vs. Peak Response Discrepancy

### 6.1 Verified Implementation Differences
1. **Energy Decomposition Mismatch:**  
   Pandey & Kumar explicitly state that they use the **anisotropic strain energy decomposition of Miehe et al. [32]**. In contrast, local subroutine `f42_mixed_uel.for` implements Molnár & Gravouil (2017) isotropic degradation of the mechanical stiffness tensor and an un-split deviatoric driving energy. Under tensile Mode-I loading with plane-strain Poisson contraction, Miehe's spectral split degrades tensile principal directions while preserving compressive energy, whereas isotropic degradation degrades all stress components equally. This represents a confirmed theoretical and numerical divergence.
2. **Discretization Topology & Transition Grading:**  
   The primary paper's Standard PFM mesh contains **26,282 elements** (mixed quads and triangles), whereas the local structured grid contains **15,192 pure quadrilateral elements**. Although both share $h = 0.003\,\mathrm{mm}$ in the crack corridor, the transition zoning to the coarse outer boundary ($h = 0.02\,\mathrm{mm}$) is entirely different, altering far-field compliance and stress concentration at the seam tip.
3. **Imposed Loading Rate & Increment Strategy:**  
   The paper's reported text specifies $\Delta u_1 = 10^{-4}$ in 2000 increments to $u = 0.005\,\mathrm{mm}$ via tabular amplitudes, whereas the local deck specifies fixed step time $\Delta t = 5\times 10^{-4}$ ($\Delta u = 2.5\times 10^{-6}\,\mathrm{mm}$).

### 6.2 Cause Attribution Assessment
**Can any single verified implementation mismatch be proven to explain the peak load difference?**  
**NO.** While the energy split and mesh topology differences are verified, their quantitative contributions to the peak load offset cannot be isolated without running controlled parameter variations (e.g., implementing Miehe's spectral projection in the UEL, or constructing the exact 26,282 mixed quad/tri mesh). In accordance with epistemic discipline, asserting that any single factor causes the offset is scientifically indefensible.  
**Verdict:** The underlying cause remains **`UNRESOLVED`** (`UNRESOLVED_PUBLISHED_CURVE_OFFSET`).

